WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.230
Somehow, in movies filled with larger-than-life,
super-powered characters, the totally regular

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.140
dude with the bow and arrow still managed
to become a fan favorite.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.280
It's pretty unlikely, when you think about
it, that Clint Barton better known by his

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.620
superheroic alias of Hawkeye would ever become
so popular.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.999
But that's exactly what happened, and his
upcoming return to the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.050
in Avengers: Endgame is generating tons of
hype among the MCU's most ardent fans.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.840
Who cares about that whole "beat Thanos and
resurrect half the universe's population"

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.340
thing?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.480
Hawkeye's back, and he's got a new outfit!

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.190
"None of this makes sense."

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.540
But sometimes what a character wears in a
movie is nearly as important as what the character

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.820
actually does, and Hawkeye's no different.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.620
That's why it's interesting to imagine an
alternate universe where Hawkeye had a different

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.560
look on the big screen.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.620
For that thought exercise, we turn to our
exclusive interview with Charlie Wen, former

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:54.460
head of visual development at Marvel Studios,
who dropped some knowledge about the direction

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.780
he and his team almost went when designing
Hawkeye's threads.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.040
Believe it or not, Hawkeye originally got
started in the masked adventuring game not

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.350
because he wanted to make a difference in
the world, not because of some kind of trauma

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.440
that forced him into a life of archery, and
not because he was bitten by a radioactive

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:09.940
arrow.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.620
No, it was because he was working at a carnival
sideshow and got jealous of how much people

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:15.640
loved Iron Man.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.420
That's seriously it.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.600
Now, with that kind of origin story one in
which a guy decides to be a costumed crimefighter

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.290
purely out of spite it's no surprise that
the first version of the outfit he put together

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.920
leaves a lot to be desired.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.240
That original outfit eventually morphed into
the more or less classic blue and purple costume

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.900
that the character is best known for.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.320
But let's be real: neither of these outfits
ever had any hope of making it onto the big

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.240
screen in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.899
One of Hawkeye's most influential changes
occured in 2002, when Mark Millar and Bryan

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.620
Hitch launched The Ultimates sort of a modern
version of the original Avengers team.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.580
Ultimate Hawkeye wears a sort of tactical
espionage outfit and a pair of shades.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.620
There isn't anything particularly iconic or
even memorable about it, but it does look

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.360
much more realistic… and far less ridiculous
than his traditional comic book purple.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.010
Charlie Wen explained that when designing
Hawkeye's cinematic appearances in Thor and

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:15.840
Avengers, the art from the first two Ultimates
runs penciled by Bryan Hitch, served as an

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.070
overall inspiration.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:23.580
"I know we definitely referenced a lot of
Bryan Hitch's work on the Ultimates."

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.800
Wen explained that the work he was doing for
Avengers was a departure from the comic books

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.130
we all grew up reading even though the movies
of the MCU are well known for how close they

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.580
stuck to the source material.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.120
"The kind of stories that I was telling was
definitely a lot more grown up stories, right?

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.380
Not the kind that we grew up with.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.910
So were the costumes, it was a little bit
more gritty, a little bit more earthy."

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:51.900
“In Avengers, I was creating the Hawkeye
for something that was more urban.”

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.640
"It's not like he was in this whole superhero
world at all.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
He's a normal guy."

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.480
About a year after The Avengers hit theaters,
another long-awaited superhero project started

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.700
gathering steam: the Netflix series Daredevil,
starring Charlie Cox.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.420
And while Wen didn't actually work on the
Daredevil project it seems that great minds

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.640
really do think alike.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.880
According to Wen, he thought his approach
to Clint Barton's cinematic design managed

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.610
to find its way on-screen anyway, just in
the form of Netflix's take on Matt Murdock.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.980
"So In a way, maybe I was envisioning a little
more of what eventually became Daredevil,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.090
for the Netflix series much later.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.140
That's kind of the feel that I was thinking
about when I was doing that version of Hawkeye."

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:40.390
Crucial to Wen's approach, he said, were the
qualities that definitively make Hawkeye less

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.390
"super" and more "human":

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:46.350
"You know, I wanted to make sure that I also
portrayed a version that was more urban and

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.040
more about a family guy.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.520
Who is Hawkeye as a person?"

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.500
That vision of the character eventually appeared
along with Hawkeye's wife and kids by the

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.140
time Avengers: Age of Ultron hit theaters
in 2015.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.900
Funnily enough, that movie featured a costume
design that hewed even more closely to Hawkeye's

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.860
classic, non-Ultimate comic book design.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.220
Throughout the character's existence, not
only has Clint Barton changed outfits with

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.680
some regularity, he's even changed identities.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.340
In the '70s, he got mixed up with Hank Pym's
size-changing gizmos and took on the identity

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.080
of Goliath.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.880
And in the 2000s, Hawkeye took on yet another
super-alias: Ronin, a black-clad martial artist

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.360
who's as good at using a sword as he is a
bow and arrow.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.440
Meanwhile, the MCU's version of Hawkeye seems
to have been fully reinvented yet again.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:35.490
The trailers for 2019's Avengers: Endgame
revealed that Clint Barton seems to have shed

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:40.540
his Hawkeye identity in favor of what appears
to be the cinematic debut of the Ronin outfit.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.590
While we don't yet have much information about
what may have prompted the character's latest

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.270
change of clothes, it goes without saying
that Wen's work designing a grounded, realistic

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.440
version of Clint Barton undoubtedly had a
huge influence on the Hawkeye we've grown

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.540
to know and love.

